From 72ce48d5068f48b33234f0e6891a05859a0b0c65 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Guap <allfantasysportsapp@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 21:03:12 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Every App Router route shipped HTML with no document shell,
 and a white page
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Next's Google Fonts optimizer is on by default. It rewrites rendered HTML to
hoist <link rel=stylesheet href=fonts.googleapis.com...> into <head> and inline
the CSS. The root layout puts those links INSIDE <body> deliberately — the
comment there explains a <link> at that position is the one place stylesheet
ordering is guaranteed, after an @import in af-core.css was measured being
dropped when another af-*.css concatenated ahead of it.

The optimizer does not cope with that placement. It strips the <link> and takes
<!DOCTYPE>, <html>, <head> and <body> with it.

Measured against production before this change: `/`, `/login`, `/pricing`,
`/privacy` and the not-found route all began at `<meta charSet="utf-8"/>` with
no shell — 54,609 bytes of real content, no wrapper. `/500` came through with a
correct `<!DOCTYPE html><html><head>`; it is the one Pages Router route and has
no Google Fonts link. That split is the whole diagnosis: the corruption tracks
the font link, not the router, not the host.

Downstream, the browser parses the fragment in QUIRKS MODE and synthesises a
bare <body> — without `className="antialiased min-h-screen mode-readable"` and
without `style={{background:'var(--bg)',color:'var(--text)'}}`. Those two
tokens are what give the page a background and a text colour, so the content
mounts and renders invisibly. 11 child nodes present, nothing on screen. React
then fails to hydrate against a tree that does not match what it emitted, retries
a client render, fails again and throws AggregateError from the scheduler,
unmounting what was there.

Nothing about this was host-specific. It reproduced identically on the Railway
service domain and on www.allfantasy.ai, with every CSS chunk, JS chunk and API
call returning 200, on a cold build with no cache reuse. It has been present for
as long as the font links have been in the layout, which is why the production
domain has never rendered.

Turning the optimizer off leaves the links where the layout puts them and the
stylesheet ordering the comment protects. The fonts still load from Google; they
are simply not inlined at build time, which costs one request and no correctness.

The alternative — moving the links into <head> — was not taken. That is the
placement the layout comment explicitly argues against, and it would trade a
document-shell bug for the font-ordering bug it was written to prevent.
---
 next.config.js | 20 ++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+)

diff --git a/next.config.js b/next.config.js
index 9a011c34..bc85c535 100644
--- a/next.config.js
+++ b/next.config.js
@@ -18,6 +18,26 @@ const isRailwayRuntime = !!(
 
 const nextConfig = {
   reactStrictMode: true,
+
+  // Next's built-in Google Fonts optimizer rewrites the rendered HTML to hoist and
+  // inline <link> tags pointing at fonts.googleapis.com into <head>. The root layout
+  // places those links INSIDE <body> on purpose (see the comment in app/layout.tsx
+  // about stylesheet ordering), and the optimizer mangles the document trying to move
+  // them: the <link> is stripped from the output and <!DOCTYPE>/<html>/<head>/<body>
+  // go with it.
+  //
+  // Measured on production 2026-08-26: every App Router route served 54KB of HTML
+  // beginning at `<meta charSet="utf-8"/>` with no document shell, while /500 — the
+  // one Pages Router route, which has no Google Fonts link — was emitted correctly.
+  // The browser then falls into quirks mode and synthesises a bare <body> without the
+  // `mode-readable` class or the inline background/color that define --bg/--text, so
+  // the content renders invisibly; the same shell mismatch fails hydration with an
+  // AggregateError. Net effect: a blank white page on every route.
+  //
+  // Disabling the optimizer leaves the links exactly where the layout puts them. The
+  // fonts still load from Google, just not inlined at build time.
+  optimizeFonts: false,
+
   distDir: process.env.AF_NEXT_DIST_DIR || (isProd ? '.next' : '.next-dev-local'),
 
   // Skip in-build type-check and lint passes — they OOM in Vercel's build container
-- 
